---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const siteUrl = Astro.site?.toString().replace(/\/$/, "") || "https://kugou9.com";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About the Index - Who Sang It First? | Global Song Index"
  description="Learn about the Global Song Index methodology, how original performers are identified, how international covers are catalogued, and our open music data sources."
  keywords="about global song index, music catalogue methodology, who sang it first guide, cover song research, musicbrainz attributions"
  schema={{
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "AboutPage",
    name: "About Global Song Index",
    url: `${siteUrl}/about/`,
    description: "Methodology and mission of the Global Song Index international music catalog."
  }}
>
  <div class="page-shell narrow-page" style="max-width: 820px;">
    <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">Home</a>
      <i data-lucide="chevron-right"></i>
      <span>About</span>
    </nav>

    <header class="page-header">
      <p class="eyebrow"><i data-lucide="info"></i> About The Project</p>
      <h1>About Global Song Index</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        An open music encyclopedia dedicated to tracking who sang it first, preserving original recording credits, and mapping the cross-border journey of cover versions across 130+ nations.
      </p>
    </header>

    <article class="prose" style="padding: 44px 0 80px;">
      <h2>Our Mission: Who Sang It First?</h2>
      <p>
        Every great song has an origin story. While many timeless tracks are popularized by famous cover artists decades later—such as Whitney Houston's soaring performance of Dolly Parton's <em>"I Will Always Love You"</em> or Jeff Buckley's emotional interpretation of Leonard Cohen's <em>"Hallelujah"</em>—audiences and music researchers frequently seek to understand the original vocalist and the historical context of the first recording.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Global Song Index</strong> solves this question by documenting the chronological release records of songs and establishing verified attributions for original performers alongside global adaptations.
      </p>

      <h2>Catalogue Classification Methodology</h2>
      <p>
        Songs and artist performances in our database are categorized into three verified roles:
      </p>
      <div class="faq-grid" style="grid-template-columns: 1fr; margin: 20px 0 32px;">
        <div class="faq-card">
          <h3 class="faq-question">
            <span class="role-badge original" style="margin-right: 6px;"><i data-lucide="check-circle-2"></i> Original</span> First Commercial Recording
          </h3>
          <p class="faq-answer">
            The earliest documented studio or commercial live audio recording released by the performing artist or group that introduced the musical composition to the public.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-card">
          <h3 class="faq-question">
            <span class="role-badge cover" style="margin-right: 6px;">Cover</span> Cover Version & Adaptation
          </h3>
          <p class="faq-answer">
            Any subsequent performance or recording of the song by another artist or ensemble. This includes acoustic covers, genre transformations (e.g. folk to rock), and international language adaptations.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-card">
          <h3 class="faq-question">
            <span class="role-badge unknown" style="margin-right: 6px;">Unconfirmed</span> Research in Progress
          </h3>
          <p class="faq-answer">
            Recordings currently undergoing historical verification where chronological priority or performance lineage is being cross-referenced against historical archives.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>Geographical & Country Attribution</h2>
      <p>
        Unlike regional streaming platforms that index songs by publisher territory, Global Song Index indexes recordings by the <strong>origin nation or territory of the performing artist</strong>. A song recorded in 1965 by a British band and later covered by artists in France, Japan, and Brazil appears cross-indexed in each corresponding country catalogue.
      </p>

      <h2>Data Sources & Verification</h2>
      <p>
        Our catalogue synthesizes data from the community-driven <a href="https://musicbrainz.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="color: var(--navy); font-weight: 700; text-decoration: underline;">MusicBrainz</a> open knowledge base, national discographical libraries, and verified public historical records. Data is continuously updated, normalized, and deployed globally via Cloudflare D1 distributed edge storage.
      </p>

      <h2>Explore the Catalogue</h2>
      <p>
        Ready to discover music history?
      </p>
      <div style="display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-top: 18px; flex-wrap: wrap;">
        <a href="/countries/" class="button primary">
          <i data-lucide="globe-2"></i> Browse Countries
        </a>
        <a href="/search/" class="button">
          <i data-lucide="search"></i> Search Songs & Artists
        </a>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .prose h2 {
    font-size: 24px;
    margin-top: 36px;
    margin-bottom: 12px;
  }
  .prose p {
    color: var(--ink-secondary);
    line-height: 1.75;
    font-size: 15.5px;
    margin-bottom: 18px;
  }
</style>
